# Model & tool policy

A small, reusable policy so model/tool choice is a default, not a per-task improvisation.

## Match the model to the work

| Work type | Model | Reasoning |
|---|---|---|
| Planning, architecture, expanding a phase into gated units, design trade-offs | strongest available | high |
| Executing an ambiguous or cross-cutting unit (schema, auth, multi-system refactor) | strongest available | high |
| Debugging anything gnarly (cheap-model attempts have failed or the bug spans systems) | strongest available | high |
| Executing a well-specified plan unit | fast/mid model | medium |
| Mechanical edits (rename, move, formatting, doc sync) | fast model | low |
| Cheap classification / lightweight in-app helpers | smallest model | low |

The spec quality sets the model floor: a tight unit spec with a clear gate makes cheap execution safe; an underdetermined unit means decisions get made during execution, so use the strong model even though it's "just execution." If the reverse cost is high (a `DECISIONS.md`-worthy call), the model cost is noise.

## Where the choice gets made in continuous mode

The tier is decided when the unit is planned, not when it's dispatched. `/plan-feature` ends the plan with a **Model:** line (`fast`, `mid`, or `strong`, with one clause of reasoning) using the table above; the human sees it at approval. Whoever dispatches honors it:

- **Linear → Cursor cloud agent:** put `[model=<id>]` in the delegation comment (`@Cursor build this per plans/features/SS-42-*.md [model=…]`). Set a sensible dashboard default too, but don't rely on it — Linear-triggered agents have been observed ignoring it.
- **Coordinator (Cursor Project):** picks the model per subagent from the plan's tier.
- **You, driving directly:** Auto is fine for `fast`/`mid`; pick the strongest model explicitly for `strong`.
- **Claude Code:** set the session model (desktop model picker or `/model`) from the tier — `fast` → Haiku, `mid` → Sonnet, `strong` → Opus (current generation; as of Sep 2026: Haiku 4.5 / Sonnet 5 / Opus 5). Raise effort for `strong`. Subagents take a `model:` in their frontmatter the same way.
- **Tracker labels:** keep the tier as a Linear label group (`model` → `fast` / `mid` / `strong`) so exactly one applies per issue.

Default tier for a well-specified unit is `mid`. Anything touching prompts that ship to users, safety behavior, schema, auth, or a `DECISIONS.md` call is `strong` regardless of size.

## Escalate on first failure

If a fast/mid model whiffs a unit once, hand it to the strongest model — don't re-prompt the same tier. The second cheap attempt has worse odds than the first (it's now working around its own mess, or the spec was underdetermined), and two failed cheap runs plus review time cost more than one strong run.

## Planning is a reviewed deliverable, not a side effect

When expanding a phase: write the plan into `plans/<phase>/` with entry deps + verification gates **and stop**. A human reviews/approves before any execution agent builds. Don't let a planning run drift into implementation.

## Parallelism

- Parallelize **only independent** units (neither's entry dependency includes the other).
- Anything with a chain dependency runs **serially**: build → verify gate → update `docs/status.md` + landing pad → PR → next.
- Prefer one deliberate action + verification over speculative parallel edits to the same files.

## Subagents

- Use a specialized subagent with restricted tools for a scoped job (e.g. read-only `code-review`).
- Keep subagents aligned with `AGENTS.md`; a stale subagent is worse than none.
- Give a subagent the full context it needs — it doesn't see the parent conversation.

## Gates must be phone-checkable

Every plan unit's gate item is tagged `[CI]` / `[ARTIFACT]` / `[MANUAL]`. Favor `[CI]`. See `plans/README.md`.
